<!--Post: 1058--><!--Time: 1096181510-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1058"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Verm_inve<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Verm_inve<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, September 25, 2004 - 11:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/local/crime_courts/9761822.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/local/crime_ courts/9761822.htm</a> <BR> <BR>Likely not a BTK victim, but she was mentioned in the article.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1058-->
<!--Post: 1059--><!--Time: 1096205087-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1059"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, September 26, 2004 - 6:24 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Don&#39;t count BTK out on this one. I don&#39;t think that he cares for mental health professionals. They could not or did not help him. He is very capable of changing his MO. That is how he leads the cops astray. Alot of people get off on fire setting. Serial killers mature at their craft and accomodate their methods as they refine their skills. Whatever works. It is the thrill of the kill that counts. <BR> <BR>I am assuming that this is a small and perhaps more rural community, just like Jake Allen&#39;s locale. Or Kansas is really turning out alot of killers.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1059-->
<!--Post: 1061--><!--Time: 1096257189-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1061"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, September 26, 2004 - 8:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->A friend of mine lives in Park City, KS and says south of 45th St is Wichita and north of 45th Street is Park City. So 45th Street of Wichita is the city limits of Park City. If you go to Yahoo Maps and put 45th St. in the street address and Park City, KS in the town/state section &#40;which is the Wichita city limits&#41; you will get a point on the map. Put in Benton, KS as the next location and it is approximately 13 miles. So Benton, KS, although it is in the next county to Wichita, is only 13 miles away from Wichita. It is that close. <BR>-Deb<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1061-->
<!--Post: 1062--><!--Time: 1096292739-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1062"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, September 27, 2004 - 6:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks for the location info. Suburban areas are even more convenient for BTK. I had an aunt that lived in Parsons and that is about my geographical knowledge of Kansas.  Never assume anything.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1062-->
<!--Post: 1065--><!--Time: 1096379468-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1065"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 28, 2004 - 6:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The newspaper articles and TV reports stated that this area was rural. It has been ruled a homicide. Things were taken from her purse and one item with her name was found outside the house. They were shocked that someone would try this in the daytime, which is yet another police blinder. Crime is a 24 hour a day thing. This has the marks of BTK. He definitely has a job or lifestyle that allows him tremendous mobility and alot of cover. She also worked in her husband&#39;s office, which would provide some visibility to her and associate her with a mental health professional. I hope that the autopsy provides some very clear info and leads. This has the earmarks of BTK, and he will be pissed if someone is imitating him. The more police jurisdictions involved in the BTK case, the less likely he will be caught. <BR> <BR>There was also an article about a 7th body being dumped in Kansas City, all in the same area. The police are wondering if all the bodies are related in some way! Definitely a case for Sherlock Holmes.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1065-->
<!--Post: 1066--><!--Time: 1096392657-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1066"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 28, 2004 - 10:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The Mould case is rural, but there were houses on either side of hers and her neighbors are pretty shook up. Because of the fire, she had burns on 90 percent of her body. According to the local paper, the El Dorado Times, the WPD homicide investigators went to the scene to help out. Butler County Sheriff Craig Murphy said &#34;If I need their help they&#39;ll be there&#34; again, he said, adding &#34;they are simply doing their job&#34; and would be &#34;very remiss&#34; if they were not concerned about any Butler County homicide case. &#34;I have had a working relationship with them for years,&#34; he said. &#34;I have always gone to them for help and they have always come over to help me. &#34;It&#39;s a team effort; it takes all of us.&#34; Murphy said &#34;there is absolutely nothing to indicate there is any connection&#34; between the Mould case and Wichita&#39;s BTK cases. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.eldoradotimes.com/articles/2004/09/28/news/news1.txt" target=_top>http://www.eldoradotimes.com/articles/2004/09/28/n ews/news1.txt</a> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1066-->
<!--Post: 1067--><!--Time: 1096434323-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1067"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Colette<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Colette<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 28, 2004 - 10:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Teardrop, that 7th body in Kansas City refers to the possible prostitute murders in a 20 block area. They have a suspect in custody, a guy just out on parole. The Mold murder could be BTK, maybe he wanted to burn up any forensic evidence. In his last letter BTK made some sort of threat... maybe it was to kill again.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1067-->
<!--Post: 1071--><!--Time: 1096504725-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1071"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, September 29, 2004 - 5:38 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Colette: <BR>Thank you for the info. The article did not give any details. The Mould murder could be BTK. Fire may not be his thing, but it would create another confusing issue. BTK does not seem to worry about evidence left behind. No one so far has been able to do anything with what he did leave behind. Maybe the British psychic can lend more help to the case. <BR> <BR>Meanwhile, as the police sit on the info they have, they are insuring that more people may die by the hand of BTK. Thanks again for the info. I also read in an Oklahoma site that they are having serial killer issues as well.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1071-->
<!--Post: 1073--><!--Time: 1096516903-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1073"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Colette<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Colette<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, September 29, 2004 - 9:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK didn&#39;t worry about evidence left behind before the advances in DNA. Maybe now he doesn&#39;t want people to know he is really active. There would be a bigger push to find an active killer than one sending letters out about things he&#39;s done in the past.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1073-->
<!--Post: 1076--><!--Time: 1096638527-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1076"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ittybittygal<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ittybittygal<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->9-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, October 01, 2004 - 6:48 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I live in Benton. It is not far from Wichita. The whole town is &#34;rural&#34;. The total population here is only about 800 people&#40;that does include the children&#41;. It takes me 15 minutes to get from my house to WSU going down 254 highway. <BR>Carol Moulds husband,by the way is a psychologist. Maybe it was one of his patients?  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1076-->
<!--Post: 1118--><!--Time: 1097209581-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1118"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, October 07, 2004 - 9:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Does anyone have any info on Mildred McDowell &#40;age 75&#41; and her daughter Janet? They were murdered around Benton on 5-5-91? The case is too old to get any information without going to newspaper archives, etc. I was curious as to the resolution of the case and case details. Thanks.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1118-->
<!--Post: 1121--><!--Time: 1097259281-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1121"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, October 08, 2004 - 11:14 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Nothing new in the Mould case yet. Teardrop asked about the McDowells. To search the news archives at kansas.com is easy and the search is always free. To view the full text of an article there is a fee of $2.95, but you can read the introductory paragraph and see if it is the one you wanted. Also, the information you seek may be enough in the intro paragraph that you don&#39;t need to pay the fee.  <BR> <BR>Checking the archives this afternoon, I typed in three words in the Find Articles box: McDowell murder Benton. Because you said the murders occurred in 1991, I went to the More Options section and clicked on the year in that box. Eight articles came up and one of them says that a man was charged for the murders and was in jail. I hit the back button and changed the year to 1992, but had to type in the three words all over again because they were not retained. For 1992 there are three articles. The guy was convicted and got three life terms, with no parole for 49 years.   <BR>-Deb <BR> <BR>Here is the link to start your search: <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/archives/#form" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/archives/#form</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1121-->
<!--Post: 1197--><!--Time: 1097672107-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1197"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxtrotnem<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxtrotnem<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 13, 2004 - 5:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I remember the mildred mcdowell case. as i recall some guy came down from emporia and burglarized their home. he came back after they&#39;d bought new stuff and burglarized them again so the third time they didn&#39;t replace the tv and stuff. i think he came back and surprised them at home. i heard they were chased down the hall and shot in the bathroom as they hid from him. they caught the guy and he&#39;s in prison. i don&#39;t remember if he knew the victims or not.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1197-->
<!--Post: 1205--><!--Time: 1097763378-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1205"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, October 14, 2004 - 7:16 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, Foxtrot:  I thought it unusual that their deaths would be random. At least there was a standing relationship on the part of the killer. Criminals do return to the scene of the crime, no matter how &#39;minor&#39; the event may be. I caught some vandals that way once upon a life in a  subdivision. They just had to come back and relive the event. Thanks again.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1205-->
<!--Post: 1233--><!--Time: 1097850308-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1233"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:hackenmaster@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Wichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, October 15, 2004 - 7:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is the most current article on Carol Moulde: <BR> <BR>October 7 - Investigators are still waiting for autopsy results before releasing how 43 year old Carol Mould was killed, but the investigation is moving forward. <BR> <BR>It&#39;s a crime that shook the small Benton community. Today deputies literally hit the streets to try and track down her killer. <BR> <BR>&#34;We want to catch this guy.&#34; <BR> <BR>It&#39;s been two weeks since the body of Carol Mould was found badly burned inside her Benton home. On Wednesday deputies took to the highway in hopes of learning more about how and why the mother of three was murdered. <BR> <BR>&#34;The deputies came down and stopped traffic in both directions,&#34; said Undersheriff Roy Raney of Butler County. <BR> <BR>From noon till two on Wednesday deputies stopped traffic along highway 254. It was during that same time period on September 22 when investigators believe Carol Mould was murdered. <BR> <BR>When officers arrived, there were two separate fires blazing in the home, one in the garage the other in the living room where Mould&#39;s body was found. It was possibly a failed attempt to try and cover up the crime. Investigators hope someone driving by could lead investigators to the person responsible for taking Carol Mould&#39;s life. <BR> <BR>&#34;Whether they were driving by that day or they heard something they would like for us to know about, if they could call,&#34; says Raney. <BR> <BR>Wow, they shut down a major highway on the same time frame and day 2 weeks later to hand out fliers, and talk to any regular passer/byers.   <BR> <BR>If you all need me to post the link of my resources, please let me know. <BR> <BR>wichitan<!--/Text--></td></tr>
