<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1445"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, November 24, 2004 - 10:14 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It is my very suspicious nature that does not buy the &#39;we don&#39;t know&#39; autopsy reports in the news. The exterior of her body may have been badly burned. However, that still leaves the brain and eyeballs &#40;hemorrages&#41;, vitreous fluid of the eyes, lungs to indicate alive/dead at time of fires, and on and on. It is very difficult to destroy something that is over 90% water without a very hot fire, even with an accelerant. <BR> <BR>There are too many similarities with other deaths to make this a mere random killing. It is possible that some pervert just happened by and spied a victim. But it is owed to all victims of crimes to search without ceasing until an answer is found. Again, &#39;hush, hush&#39; seems to be the current approach.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
